10. How is the transaction propagates to the bitcoin network?
a) by sending the information to peer nodes
b) by broadcasting it directly to all miners simultaneously
c) by storing it locally on the blockchain
d) by uploading it to the registry
correct: a)
explanation: When a user creates and signs a transaction in Bitcoin, it is broadcast to the
network by sending/relaying the transaction data to connected peer nodes via a P2P (peer-to-peer) gossip protocol.
Those nodes then validate and relay it to their own peers until it reaches the entire network.

11. What must happen before a transaction can be added to the mempool?
a) it must be validated by other nodes by checking the public key
b) it must be validated by other nodes by checking the bitcoin address
c) it must be validated by other nodes by checking the transaction value
d) it must be validated by other nodes by checking the signature
correct: d)
explanation: Before a node accepts a transaction into its mempool, it must cryptographically verify that
the person sending the funds actually owns them. It does this by checking the digital signature 
included in the transaction input against the corresponding public key/script. If the signature 
is valid, it proves authorization without exposing the private key; if the signature is invalid,
the node instantly rejects the transaction.

12. What do other nodes use to validate the signature of a transaction?
a) they use the secret key and signature
b) they verify the hashed public key and the signature
c) they verify the public key and the signature
d) they verify the transaction history of the BTC
correct: c) 
Explanation: To validate a digital signature, nodes do not need (and never see) your 
secret private key. Instead, they use the public key (which is revealed in the transaction 
when you spend funds) along with the signature to cryptographically verify that the signature
was generated by the corresponding private key.

