25. Why is the scripting language not Turing complete?
a) it has predicatble execution times
b) it has limited complexity (e.g. no loops)
c) it is too complex
d) it is not a deterministic process
correct: a) b)
explanation: Turing completeness would require things like loops and 
arbitrary branching. Bitcoin Script deliberately leaves out loops and similar
constructs, so it is not Turing complete. That limitation is the design choice the
question asks about. a) consequence of having no loops, and it is the reason 
for the design (nodes can know a script will terminate, so no one can freeze the 
network with an infinite loop).

26. Which statement about Bitcoin address types is correct?
a) P2PKH addresses start with the number 3 and accept arbitrary Bitcoin scripts
b) P2SH addresses start with the number 1 and always use the standard public key hash script
c) P2SH addresses start with the number 3 and accept transactions with arbitrary Bitcoin scripts
d) P2PKH and P2SH addresses both start with the number 1
correct: c)
explanation:
P2SH (Pay to Script Hash) addresses start with the number 3 and allow outputs
to be locked with arbitrary Bitcoin scripts. P2PKH (Pay to Public Key Hash) addresses 
start with the number 1 and correspond to the standard script (hash of the public key plus 
signature check). The prefixes in a) and b) are swapped, and d) is wrong because the two types
have different prefixes.


27. Why does a P2PKH transaction not require the sender to write a script manually?
a) because P2PKH transactions don’t use scripts at all
b) because Bitcoin software fills in the standard script automatically
c) because the script is stored in the address
d) because miners write the script
correct: b)
explanation:
For P2PKH, the script follows a standard template. The wallet software constructs it from the 
receiver’s address (by decoding the pubkey hash) and validates it using the standard procedure, 
so users never need to write script code themselves. Option c) is wrong because the address only
encodes the pubkey hash, not the script itself.

